WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.060
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Writing Skills.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.607
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.907 --> 00:00:14.067
Unit eight: Writing a Narrative. Today you will tell a story in the past tense.

4
00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:16.973
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:21.329
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.183 --> 00:00:23.080
Narrative.

7
00:00:24.083 --> 00:00:26.443
Narrative means a story told in order.

8
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:28.469
Write a short narrative.

9
00:00:29.083 --> 00:00:31.666
Your turn. Say: Narrative.

10
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.373
Narrative.

11
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:37.257
Sequence.

12
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.528
Sequence means the order of events.

13
00:00:41.117 --> 00:00:42.559
Keep the sequence clear.

14
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.524
Your turn. Say: Sequence.

15
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:49.507
Sequence.

16
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.181
Suddenly.

17
00:00:52.183 --> 00:00:54.868
Suddenly means in a quick and surprising way.

18
00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:57.286
Suddenly it began to rain.

19
00:00:57.883 --> 00:01:00.687
Your turn. Say: Suddenly.

20
00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:04.395
Suddenly.

21
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:05.954
Finally.

22
00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.576
Finally means at the end after a long time.

23
00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:11.602
Finally, we arrived.

24
00:01:12.217 --> 00:01:14.497
Your turn. Say: Finally.

25
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:18.183
Finally.

26
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:19.881
Character.

27
00:01:20.883 --> 00:01:23.127
Character means a person in a story.

28
00:01:23.717 --> 00:01:25.414
The main character is brave.

29
00:01:26.017 --> 00:01:28.669
Your turn. Say: Character.

30
00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:32.256
Character.

31
00:01:33.017 --> 00:01:33.774
Setting.

32
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:37.154
Setting means the time and place of a story.

33
00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.494
The setting is a small village.

34
00:01:40.083 --> 00:01:42.503
Your turn. Say: Setting.

35
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:46.112
Setting.

36
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:47.863
Climax.

37
00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.198
Climax means the most exciting part.

38
00:01:51.783 --> 00:01:53.667
The climax came at midnight.

39
00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:57.098
Your turn. Say: Climax.

40
00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:01.062
Climax.

41
00:02:01.817 --> 00:02:02.644
Ending.

42
00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:05.928
Ending means the last part of a story.

43
00:02:06.517 --> 00:02:07.947
I like a happy ending.

44
00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:10.842
Your turn. Say: Ending.

45
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.539
Ending.

46
00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:19.123
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:19.983 --> 00:02:22.366
Visal, how can I tell a story in writing?

48
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:32.479
Use the past tense. Begin by giving the setting and the characters. Then tell the events in order and finish with an ending.

49
00:02:33.583 --> 00:02:35.095
Which time words help?

50
00:02:36.183 --> 00:02:42.423
Use first, then, after that, suddenly and finally. They show the sequence clearly.

51
00:02:43.517 --> 00:02:45.760
Which past tense should I use?

52
00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:54.889
Use the past simple for main events and the past continuous for the background. For example, it was raining when I left.

53
00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:57.476
Part three: grammar.

54
00:02:58.317 --> 00:03:01.013
Grammar focus: Past simple story.

55
00:03:01.313 --> 00:03:02.569
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:05.213
We arrived at the temple at noon.

57
00:03:06.117 --> 00:03:08.650
Suddenly, the rain started to fall.

58
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.468
Use the past simple for the main events of a story. Add time words like first, then, suddenly and finally.

59
00:03:16.217 --> 00:03:20.158
Be careful! Don't say: Yesterday we go to Angkor.

60
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.378
Say: Yesterday we went to Angkor.

61
00:03:24.678 --> 00:03:26.051
Use the past simple.

62
00:03:26.867 --> 00:03:29.484
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.603
A Day at Angkor.

64
00:03:31.903 --> 00:03:34.773
Last year Mony visited Angkor Wat with her family.

65
00:03:35.767 --> 00:03:38.683
First, they arrived at sunrise and took photos.

66
00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.990
Then they walked through the old temples.

67
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:45.232
Suddenly, it began to rain heavily.

68
00:03:46.233 --> 00:03:49.162
Finally, they sat under a tree and laughed together.

69
00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:54.373
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:03:55.217 --> 00:03:57.089
A narrative is a blank.

71
00:03:57.389 --> 00:04:00.415
A, bill. B, map. C, story.

72
00:04:03.967 --> 00:04:05.908
The answer is C: story.

73
00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:09.351
Use the blank tense for main events.

74
00:04:09.651 --> 00:04:12.887
A, past. B, future. C, never.

75
00:04:16.433 --> 00:04:18.665
The answer is A: past.

76
00:04:19.817 --> 00:04:21.654
Suddenly shows something blank.

77
00:04:21.954 --> 00:04:25.236
A, boring. B, unexpected. C, slow.

78
00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.043
The answer is B: unexpected.

79
00:04:32.183 --> 00:04:33.927
The setting is the time and blank.

80
00:04:34.227 --> 00:04:38.055
A, price. B, size. C, place.

81
00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.553
The answer is C: place.

82
00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.008
Mony visited blank.

83
00:04:46.308 --> 00:04:49.764
A, Angkor Wat. B, a bank. C, a farm.

84
00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.323
The answer is A: Angkor Wat.

85
00:04:56.533 --> 00:04:59.627
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:00.483 --> 00:05:02.018
How do you write a story?

87
00:05:10.817 --> 00:05:12.456
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:12.756 --> 00:05:22.760
I begin with the setting and the characters. I tell the events in order with time words like first and then. I use the past tense and finish with a clear ending.

89
00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:24.665
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:29.468
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and past simple story.

91
00:05:29.768 --> 00:05:34.255
Today's value is imagination. Imagine new ideas for a better future.

92
00:05:34.555 --> 00:05:36.940
See you in Unit nine. Goodbye!

93
00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.648
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

